WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:06.240
Marvel's culmination of a 22-movie journey
is so long it's hard to imagine that anything

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.320
got left on the cutting room floor or tossed
into the writers' room wastebasket.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.929
Yet there's plenty of stuff that didn't make
it on screen - here are some planned or even

00:00:14.929 --> 00:00:17.540
filmed scenes that didn't end up in Avengers:
Endgame.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.080
Tony Stark's other daughter

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:24.350
In October 2018, Marvel announced that Katherine
Langford - star of the controversial Netflix

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:28.750
drama 13 Reasons Why - shot some scenes for
the final Avengers installment.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:33.809
Who exactly she portrayed was kept secret
so as to reveal as little about the plot of

00:00:33.809 --> 00:00:35.120
the movie as possible.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.620
But fans were left hanging when they actually
saw Endgame, because Langford was nowhere

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:41.620
to be found.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:46.320
Only after half of the world saw the movie
did directors Anthony and Joe Russo reveal

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.420
that Langford had been hired to play an older
version of Tony Stark and Pepper Potts' young

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:52.530
daughter, Morgan.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:56.710
The Russos shot a dreamlike scene - which
would have mirrored the one featuring Thanos

00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:02.219
and Gamora from Infinity War - in which Tony
would've met the young adult version of Morgan,

00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:03.469
portrayed by Langford.

00:01:03.469 --> 00:01:08.520
Joe Russo explained the idea for the axed
scene in an interview with the Happy Sad Confused

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.080
podcast a few weeks after Endgame premiered:

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:15.320
"The intention was that his future daughter
- because these are the stones we're dealing

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.330
with, and they're magic - his future daughter
forgave him and sort of gave him peace to

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:20.330
go."

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:25.900
The scene got cut, however, because we'd never
actually seen this version of Morgan, and

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.870
it was just too much in a movie as overstuffed
as Avengers: Endgame:

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:35.550
"We showed it to test audiences and it was
really confusing."

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:36.550
Even more fighting

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:41.340
The climactic battle of Avengers: Endgame
is one of the most complicated superhero slugfests

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:42.670
ever put to film.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:48.060
After Thanos violently confronts the Avengers
to reclaim his Infinity Stones, nearly every

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:53.060
living hero in the vast Marvel universe comes
together to defeat the Mad Titan once and

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:54.060
for all.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.960
The sequence is as long as it is ambitious,
taking up most of the final third of the movie.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.200
But if the screenwriters' initial plans had
come to fruition, that fight for the ages

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.540
could've been bigger.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:08.229
According to a New York Times interview with
the movie's co-writers, Christopher Markus

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:13.230
and Stephen McFeely, the battle itself would've
had a three-act structure all its own.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.769
But when all was said and done, it was overkill.

00:02:15.769 --> 00:02:21.939
Case in point, among the cuts was a scene-within-the-scene,
falling during a three-minute ceasefire.

00:02:21.939 --> 00:02:23.269
McFeely explained that one:

00:02:23.269 --> 00:02:27.760
"It didn’t play well, but we had a scene
in a trench where, for reasons, the battle

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.213
got paused for about three minutes and now
there’s 18 people all going, 'What are we

00:02:31.213 --> 00:02:32.213
going to do?'

00:02:32.213 --> 00:02:33.213
'I’m going to do this.'

00:02:33.213 --> 00:02:34.989
'I’m going to do this.'

00:02:34.989 --> 00:02:38.129
Just bouncing around this completely fake,
fraudulent scene."

00:02:38.129 --> 00:02:39.610
Trailer travails

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:44.840
Avengers: Endgame was a highly anticipated
movie, which left Marvel Studios in a tricky

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:45.840
predicament.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.670
How could it promote the movie with trailers
and not give away anything that hinted at

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:50.670
a spoiler?

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:55.499
Co-director Joe Russo explained the predicament
to the Happy Sad Confused podcast way back

00:02:55.499 --> 00:02:59.609
in May of 2018, after Avengers: Infinity War
had debuted:

00:02:59.609 --> 00:03:03.200
"We use all the materials that we have at
our disposal to create a trailer"

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.889
"Audiences are so predictive now that you
have to be very smart about how you craft

00:03:06.889 --> 00:03:09.760
a trailer because an audience can watch a
trailer and basically tell you what's gonna

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:11.040
happen in the film."

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:16.620
So when it came to Endgame, that meant using
whatever non-offending footage they had, which

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:21.150
by necessity included shots the Russos knew
would not appear in the theatrical cut of

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.150
Endgame.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:26.659
Among those scenes is this sequence of Black
Widow training for a big fight by rapidly

00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:29.189
unloading a firearm at a shooting range.

00:03:29.189 --> 00:03:33.569
Considering that Black Widow's major contribution
to the plot's outcome has little to do with

00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:38.519
her marksmanship, it's safe to say that this
footage just had no place in the final film.

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:40.200
Now, speaking of Black Widow...

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.560
The trip to Vormir could've gone differently

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.319
While Avengers: Endgame scored high marks
with critics and audiences, one of the major

00:03:46.319 --> 00:03:50.900
qualms fans have is the way in which Black
Widow makes her final exit.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:55.379
When Black Widow and Hawkeye head to the planet
Vormir to nab the Soul Stone, they learn that

00:03:55.379 --> 00:03:57.939
one of the two must die to get it.

00:03:57.939 --> 00:04:02.419
Natasha makes the ultimate sacrifice, diving
off a cliff to her demise.

00:04:02.419 --> 00:04:06.930
According to screenwriters Christopher Markus
and Stephen McFeely in the New York Times,

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.280
they first wrote the scene the other way.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:10.280
Said McFeely:

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:15.359
"Jen Underdahl, our visual effects producer,
read an outline or draft where Hawkeye goes

00:04:15.359 --> 00:04:16.359
over.

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:18.239
And she goes, 'Don't you take this away from
her.'"

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:23.349
It was Natasha's heroic moment, in other words,
and to have Hawkeye die wouldn't have made

00:04:23.349 --> 00:04:24.600
the same impact.

